The study introduces a multi‑level text classification model designed specifically for detecting depression from natural language inputs.

The proposed system integrates ontology‑based NLP with a hybrid deep‑learning architecture combining Bi‑GRU and Bi‑LSTM, improving contextual understanding of emotional cues in text.

The model demonstrates high accuracy and robustness in identifying different levels of depression severity.

Ontology integration enhances semantic interpretation, allowing the system to better capture psychological indicators and linguistic patterns associated with depressive states.

The hybrid architecture improves feature extraction, especially for long‑sequence text data, outperforming traditional machine‑learning approaches.

The research highlights the potential of advanced NLP and deep learning techniques for early mental‑health detection, supporting clinical decision‑making and digital health interventions.
